-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Seed: the nine Kiddush answers (one row per respondent, same question)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
delete from public.responsa where question_id = (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch');
insert into public.responsa
  (rabbi_slug, position, title, title_he, question_text, question_lang, question_date,
   answer_text, answer_lang, answer_date, followup_text, followup_date, note, tags, status, question_id)
values
  ('yagel', 1, 'Answered: yes', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'כן בהחלט — Yes, absolutely. Someone who missed Friday-night kiddush and makes it during the day says the night kiddush, just without Vayechulu, which belongs to the night — בדיוק כמו שהרב עשה — exactly as the kiruv rabbi did.

הוא יכול לעשות עבור הקידוש גם אם הרב כבר יצא ידי חובה מדין ערבות. ואכן כך צריך לעשות. — The leader may make kiddush for them even after fulfilling his own obligation, through the law of arvut (mutual responsibility), and that is the correct practice.

Why rarely seen: כי זה פחות מצוי — it is simply uncommon; he has never personally encountered the situation outside the kiruv world (כנראה זה מצוי בעולם הקירוב).

His hedged distinction: for someone entirely disconnected from observance and Judaism he said, in his own hesitant words: אני לא יודע, לא הייתי עושה בשבילו קידוש — “I don''t know, I wouldn''t make kiddush for him.” For someone coming closer through kiruv, yes. He did not explain the distinction further.', 'en', '2026-11-01', NULL, NULL, 'WhatsApp voice note, 1 Nov 2026. Summary; Hebrew lines verbatim.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('shalom-rosner', 2, 'Answered: yes', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'Sounds correct, I guess it''s not so common for most people to miss', 'en', '2026-11-01', NULL, NULL, 'Email, 1 Nov 2026, 3:55 PM. Full reply verbatim — one line, sent from his iPhone. Confirms the practice and attributes its rarity to most people not missing Friday-night kiddush.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('danny-nevins', 3, 'Suggests tashlumin is more an opportunity than an obligation', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'On the status of the makeup: “the SA statement of תשלומין is somewhat less than an obligation to do it again, more like an opportunity.”

Why the practice is rarely seen, two thoughts: “I''d like to say that it''s just not so common that an observant Jew misses saying or hearing kiddush on Friday night, but maybe it''s more likely that they would be hesitant to admit it!” And another possible explanation: “Kiddush is designed to be a fulfilment of the mitzvah to remember Shabbat, and we can also fulfil that with the Kiddusha Rabba text on Saturday.”

He has not seen discussion of this beyond the regular commentaries on the Shulchan Aruch, which have little to say on it — other than the Mishnah Berurah''s comment that if you do make it up, you need to have a meal. He signs off: “Hope this helps a bit. LMK if you find anything else. Best, Danny.”', 'en', '2026-11-03', NULL, NULL, 'Email, 3 Nov 2026, 9:45 PM. Summary with selected quotations. Sent to his verified address, rabbidanielnevins@gmail.com, after the first email reached a different Daniel Nevins (“I am not a rabbi. You have the wrong Daniel Nevins.”). He does not explicitly endorse or reject the practice, and does not address whether the leader may recite it for the students after fulfilling his own obligation.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('dovid-lichtenstein', 4, 'Answered: yes — two explicit halachos, with one qualification', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'לכאורה, the rabbi acted correctly, and his practice follows from two explicit halachos in Shulchan Aruch. First, Orach Chaim 271:8: one who did not make kiddush Friday night — whether בשוגג or even במזיד — may make it up throughout Shabbos day; the Rema adds that he recites the entire nusach of the nighttime kiddush, omitting ויכולו; the Mishnah Berurah there (ס״ק לח) adds that this kiddush must likewise be במקום סעודה.

Second, the rav having already been יוצא does not prevent him from being מוציא the students: the Mechaber in 273:4 permits making kiddush for others even when he is not eating with them, and the Mishnah Berurah (ס״ק יז) explicitly includes one who has already fulfilled his own obligation — “the familiar din of אף על פי שיצא מוציא, מכח ערבות.” Even ברכת הגפן may be recited on their behalf, since it is part of their chiyuv kiddush.

His one qualification: the Mechaber writes “והוא שאינם יודעים” — that those hearing kiddush do not know how to recite it themselves. Where the students are אינם בקיאים, the heter is explicit; where they can make kiddush themselves, the Mishnah Berurah (273:20) cites the more lenient opinions but indicates that לכתחילה they should recite it themselves, and the Biur Halacha there rules that בדיעבד they are יוצא in either case.

In practice: the rav should have כוונה להוציא, the students כוונה לצאת, they should hear the entire kiddush and eat their seudah there. This kiddush also serves as their kiddush for Shabbos day — no additional kiddush is necessary (Aruch HaShulchan 271:21).

Why seldom seen: “מסתבר that the circumstances simply arise infrequently in a shomer Shabbos household, where everyone has ordinarily been יוצא Friday night. In a kiruv setting, however, this can be a very relevant halacha.” He adds that even if the students davened Maariv Friday night, the chiyuv of kiddush על הכוס remains — and that the real question is whether they were actually יוצא Friday night, not merely whether they heard someone else make kiddush.', 'en', '2026-11-04', NULL, NULL, 'Email answering the Halacha Headlines contact form, 4 Nov 2026, 10:02 AM. Summary with selected quotations. Answered by email from david@lightstonegroup.com after Headlines Radio forwarded the official contact-form submission (2 Nov 2026) to him; the first email to info@halachaheadlines.com, an address not published on the site, went unanswered.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('jeremy-wieder', 5, 'Answered: yes', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'That makes perfect sense - יצא מוציא and they are חייב in what is קידוש דאורייתא.

My guess as to why the practice is so rarely seen is because people haven''t thought through the issue in the way that this rabbi did.', 'en', '2026-11-01', NULL, NULL, 'Email, 1 Nov 2026, 6:52 PM. Full reply verbatim. The leader may discharge the students'' obligation even after fulfilling his own (yatza motzi), because they are obligated in kiddush, a biblical obligation (d''oraita); the rarity explanation is explicitly his guess.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('efrem-goldberg', 6, 'Leans yes', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'Matthew''s question: “A kiruv rabbi in Chicago made full Friday night kiddush for lunch as the students hadn''t heard Friday night one. It seems to make sense, but I''ve never seen this before. What is your approach?”

Rabbi Goldberg: “Interesting. I need to look into it.” After looking into the source — Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 with the Rema, that the full nighttime kiddush is made up during the day without Vayechulu, as kiddush b''makom seudah (Matthew attached the source page with Mishnah Berurah) — he wrote: “Right! Which would point toward his approach making sense when you throw arevus into the mix.”

Matthew followed up: “In which case you would say pashut you would make full kiddush for the college kid who is at shabbos lunch and didn''t hear Friday night kiddush.” Rabbi Goldberg: “seems so”.', 'en', '2026-11-01', NULL, NULL, 'WhatsApp exchange forwarded by Matthew, 1 Nov 2026. Tentative first reaction, not a formal psak. A supplied conversation, not direct outreach — tentative throughout (“I need to look into it”, “seems so”), an initial reaction in chat, not a considered ruling.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('noam-friedman', 7, 'Answered: yes', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'He has never done this himself — “99.9% of the time the people we were hosting for Shabbat lunch did not fall into that category” — but affirms the practice: the Shulchan Aruch provides tashlumin (a makeup) for the Friday-night bracha. “I''ve never done that, but I think it''s obviously the right call.”

Why it is rarely seen, from his own hosting experience (Hillel, and home with students near a large frum community): the guests who would have needed it usually came on Friday night, so the situation rarely arose.

He raises one educational wrinkle: a guest who comes only occasionally on Friday night might walk away thinking the night and day kiddush are the same — and immediately adds “That''s not a reason not to do it.”', 'en', '2026-11-01', NULL, NULL, 'WhatsApp voice note forwarded by Matthew, 1 Nov 2026. Summary; short quotes verbatim. A supplied voice note, not direct outreach.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('effie-kleinberg', 8, 'Answered: yes', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', 'It is a fulfillment of ''zachor'' the positive mitzvah of Shabbat, so yes that is the correct thing to do to fulfill the Biblical mitzvah, it''s not tied specifically to the entrance of Shabbat. Poskim also say it counts as well for daytime kiddush and one should have that in mind.', 'en', '2026-11-02', NULL, NULL, 'WhatsApp exchange forwarded by Matthew, 2 Nov 2026. Reply verbatim, as supplied. A supplied conversation, not direct outreach; his position as reported, not independently verified. He grounds the practice in zachor — the biblical mitzvah of remembering Shabbat, not tied to Shabbat''s entrance — and notes the poskim say it also counts toward the daytime kiddush, which one should have in mind.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch')),
  ('joseph-dweck', 9, 'Answered: yes, with a distinction', NULL, 'I understand from Shulchan Aruch OC 271:8 and the Rema that someone who missed Friday-night kiddush says the full nighttime kiddush during the day, except Vayechulu. I saw a kiruv rabbi make the nighttime kiddush at Shabbat lunch for students who hadn''t heard it Friday night. Is that correct, including if the rabbi already fulfilled his own obligation, and why is this practice so rarely seen?', 'en', '2026-11-01', '“Would same apply in a synagogue? If it is the type where the people wouldn''t have made Friday night kiddush” / “Indeed! And policy wise it would make sense for said rabbi to do the full Friday night at lunchtime in such a case” / “If everyone missed, yes” / “But what if one person did, you would have ערבות, no?” / “Didn''t/doesn''t say Friday night kiddush — they aren''t religious” / “you could say it for them. But I wouldn''t say it for everyone that way”', 'en', '2026-11-03', NULL, NULL, 'Exchange pasted by Matthew, 3 Nov 2026, verbatim as supplied. A supplied conversation, not direct outreach. The line-by-line speaker split inside the paste is not confirmed; only the final line is attributed to Rabbi Dweck — one could say kiddush for those who missed it, but he would not say it that way for everyone: a distinction between saying it for the people who missed and making that the version for the whole room. No further ruling is drawn from the rest.', array['kiddush', 'shabbat']::text[], 'published', (select id from public.responsa_questions where slug = 'making-up-friday-night-kiddush-at-shabbat-lunch'));
